  "expansion": "Latent Activation Topology of Hidden Embeddings",
  "status": "planning",
  "accent": "#22e3ff",
  "accent2": "#ff8a2a",
  "code": "internal",
  "intro": {
   "problem": "The hidden layers of the classifiers used in long-lived-particle searches are rarely examined, and we do not yet know whether visualizing them is merely striking or genuinely useful, for instance as an n-dimensional heat map showing where in latent space background closure succeeds and fails.",
   "value": "If it is useful, latent maps could diagnose failures, separate training objectives that score alike, guide architecture choices in TRUSS, and tie what a network learned back to physical inputs, as related work has begun to do for jet taggers [@khot2023][@komiske2020].",
   "approach": "We treat this as exploration with an exit: map trained models in prediction space [@mao2024], compare their hidden layers [@kornblith2019][@davari2023], and trace training trajectories [@horoi2022], then run a pre-registered kill test asking whether map coordinates predict held-out closure better than hyperparameters alone.",
   "goals": "The outcome is an honest verdict. If the kill test passes, an interactive explorer whose structures are stable across seeds becomes an analysis tool; if it fails, we record that the latent picture adds nothing a metrics table does not, and stop."
  },
  "questions": [
   "Is a latent map useful at all, or only striking: does it predict anything a metrics table does not?",
   "Could an n-dimensional heat map of closure over latent space show where and why the background estimate fails?",
   "Which latent structures survive across seeds, and which are artifacts of the projection?"
  ],
